1
00:00:01,194 --> 00:00:06,553
Everyone on the team now has VS Code, GitHub Copilot, and the Claude command line.

2
00:00:06,553 --> 00:00:07,534
Powerful tools.

3
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:10,474
But not everyone wants to live in a terminal.

4
00:00:10,732 --> 00:00:11,436
Meet Lumen.

5
00:00:11,436 --> 00:00:17,324
The same AI agents and skills, behind a friendly chat window that runs on your own computer.

6
00:00:18,824 --> 00:00:20,367
Lumen greets you by name.

7
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:24,933
Pick an agent, a specialist with its own skills, right in the message box.

8
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:28,019
Or start from one of the quick prompts underneath.

9
00:00:29,187 --> 00:00:33,328
Along the left, the same rail as Microsoft 365 Copilot.

10
00:00:33,328 --> 00:00:41,987
Chat, agents, prompts, and tasks, your notebook, a guided delivery lifecycle, insights, and samples to practise on.

11
00:00:42,267 --> 00:00:45,984
Marcus is a business analyst on the Contoso CRM project.

12
00:00:45,984 --> 00:00:51,227
He needs user stories by Friday, and he has never written a prompt in his life.

13
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:57,940
So he opens the agent picker, searches for requirements, and picks the Requirements Coach.

14
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:00,568
Its suggestions appear right under the box.

15
00:01:03,886 --> 00:01:08,387
The answer streams in, like chatting with a colleague who has done this a hundred times.

16
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:19,600
User stories, Given, When, Then acceptance criteria, and the open questions to take back to the business.

17
00:01:22,702 --> 00:01:24,615
It even suggests a next step.

18
00:01:24,615 --> 00:01:25,604
One click away.

19
00:01:28,305 --> 00:01:33,355
Every reply can be retried, branched into a new chat, rated, or exported.

20
00:01:33,355 --> 00:01:35,431
Word, PowerPoint, or Markdown.

21
00:01:35,692 --> 00:01:39,059
The business wants Word, so, Save as, Word document.

22
00:01:39,059 --> 00:01:43,074
Real headings, lists, and tables, saved to his project folder.

23
00:01:46,404 --> 00:01:49,093
Next, Marcus wants a helper for test scripts.

24
00:01:49,093 --> 00:01:53,755
There are nearly forty built in agents to search, but he wants one of his own.

25
00:01:53,755 --> 00:01:55,129
One sentence is enough.

26
00:02:02,118 --> 00:02:06,911
Lumen drafts the name, the icon, the instructions, and example requests.

27
00:02:06,911 --> 00:02:09,307
It even switches on the Excel skill.

28
00:02:16,205 --> 00:02:17,574
No jargon, no setup.

29
00:02:17,574 --> 00:02:21,749
Just a new teammate, with its own suggestions, ready to chat.

30
00:02:21,749 --> 00:02:27,362
Each agent is saved as its own small file, easy to back up or pass to a colleague.

31
00:02:28,546 --> 00:02:30,839
Now meet Priya, the project manager.

32
00:02:30,839 --> 00:02:37,975
Her world is a Microsoft Project plan and a stack of Excel workbooks, and the steering committee is on Thursday.

33
00:02:43,628 --> 00:02:46,178
To try it safely, she opens Samples.

34
00:02:46,178 --> 00:02:49,224
Realistic, made up projects to practise on.

35
00:02:49,224 --> 00:02:52,482
One click sets up the project management demo.

36
00:02:56,118 --> 00:03:06,486
Here is the plan from Microsoft Project, forty two tasks with a baseline, plus a budget tracker, a RAID log, timesheets, and this week's progress update in Excel.

37
00:03:12,315 --> 00:03:13,815
She asks for a health check.

38
00:03:13,815 --> 00:03:16,816
Lumen has already picked the right plan from her folder.

39
00:03:21,139 --> 00:03:23,915
The Schedule Analyst reads the actual plan.

40
00:03:23,915 --> 00:03:28,307
Critical path, slippage against baseline, and who is over allocated.

41
00:03:30,601 --> 00:03:31,858
Amber, trending red.

42
00:03:31,858 --> 00:03:38,771
Go-live has slipped ten working days, there is no float left, and one integration developer is the bottleneck.

43
00:03:38,771 --> 00:03:42,164
It even warns that the progress data is two weeks old.

44
00:03:43,337 --> 00:03:46,159
Any table copies straight into Excel or Outlook.

45
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:50,099
And because she wants this check every week, she sets it to repeat.

46
00:03:54,769 --> 00:03:55,799
Mondays at nine.

47
00:03:55,799 --> 00:04:01,915
It waits under Tasks, and Lumen runs it while it is open, leaving the result as an unread chat.

48
00:04:10,077 --> 00:04:12,747
Friday's progress arrived as a spreadsheet.

49
00:04:12,747 --> 00:04:16,285
Instead of retyping it into Project, Priya hands it over.

50
00:04:17,926 --> 00:04:25,030
Lumen reads the workbook, updates the plan, saves a new version without touching the original, and checks the schedule again.

51
00:04:29,669 --> 00:04:32,293
Every change is listed, before and after.

52
00:04:32,293 --> 00:04:40,741
Progress is up from forty seven to fifty three percent, and it warns that the E R P delay will push go-live back another three days.

53
00:04:43,644 --> 00:04:46,418
And See changes shows exactly what moved.

54
00:04:46,418 --> 00:04:48,786
Every task field, before and after.

55
00:04:51,811 --> 00:04:54,947
When she wants a file, she lets Lumen edit, with one click.

56
00:05:01,324 --> 00:05:06,423
And gets a colour coded Excel tracker, with a Gantt chart, ready for the steering committee.

57
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:12,334
Finally, meet Dana, the delivery lead on the Online Permit Renewal program.

58
00:05:12,334 --> 00:05:17,695
Requirements, design, and stories all have to line up, and the auditors will ask how.

59
00:05:28,971 --> 00:05:32,262
The lifecycle sample sets up her program documents.

60
00:05:32,262 --> 00:05:39,424
A project definition and requirements in Word, a solution blueprint, a data model, and a traceability workbook.

61
00:05:45,223 --> 00:05:46,962
First, a traceability check.

62
00:05:46,962 --> 00:05:52,861
The Traceability Analyst reads every layer of the workbook, from business requirement to story.

63
00:05:58,787 --> 00:06:01,531
Coverage for every layer, and a red status.

64
00:06:01,531 --> 00:06:04,913
Two stories marked ready would build the wrong thing.

65
00:06:04,913 --> 00:06:09,635
One builds a withdrawn requirement, and one still uses the old photo rule.

66
00:06:09,635 --> 00:06:17,228
It also catches a link to a requirement that does not exist, a story that skips design, and conflicting sources on SMS.

67
00:06:17,228 --> 00:06:20,419
Every finding has its cell reference and an owner.

68
00:06:26,198 --> 00:06:27,960
Next, the guided lifecycle.

69
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:37,228
A workflow takes requirements through functional design, technical design, stories, and test cases, with an approval gate between every stage.

70
00:06:37,491 --> 00:06:39,884
And the workflow is hers to shape.

71
00:06:39,884 --> 00:06:43,754
She drags technical design alongside functional design.

72
00:06:43,754 --> 00:06:47,484
Now both run side by side, and stories wait for both.

73
00:06:47,484 --> 00:06:49,736
The diagram redraws as she goes.

74
00:06:54,087 --> 00:06:59,570
She names the run, picks the requirements and the project definition, and starts the first stage.

75
00:07:08,634 --> 00:07:16,207
The Requirements Refiner works in its own chat, with the source documents already pinned, and saves its output to the project folder.

76
00:07:26,298 --> 00:07:33,109
Thirty eight requirements, each with acceptance criteria and a source, all marked draft until approved.

77
00:07:33,109 --> 00:07:40,912
It spots the SMS conflict, follows the project definition, and lists the questions to take back to the business owner.

78
00:07:46,101 --> 00:07:49,353
Before signing off, an advisory traceability check.

79
00:07:49,353 --> 00:07:52,032
It reports gaps, and never changes a file.

80
00:08:07,846 --> 00:08:08,741
Dana approves.

81
00:08:08,741 --> 00:08:15,513
Her name and the time are stamped on the document, and both design stages are now ready to start together.

82
00:08:15,513 --> 00:08:20,177
Send a stage back later, and everything downstream is flagged for review.

83
00:08:24,299 --> 00:08:30,760
Every thumbs up and down lands in Insights, so the team can see which agents and prompts are earning their keep.

84
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:32,491
It all stays on this computer.

85
00:08:38,250 --> 00:08:39,719
Not sure where to start?

86
00:08:39,719 --> 00:08:41,433
Just ask Lumen about itself.

87
00:08:41,433 --> 00:08:45,717
It knows every agent, prompt, workflow, and schedule on this computer.

88
00:08:54,306 --> 00:08:59,173
Under the hood, Lumen drives the Claude command line, and it is pluggable.

89
00:08:59,173 --> 00:09:01,474
GitHub Copilot is one setting away.

90
00:09:04,179 --> 00:09:12,419
Connect Azure DevOps, and SharePoint and Microsoft 365, and agents can read work items, documents, and meetings.

91
00:09:12,419 --> 00:09:16,318
Nothing changes in Azure DevOps until you approve it.

92
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:29,194
And every agent and prompt exports to Microsoft 365 Copilot, or straight into VS Code, for the day you are ready for more.

93
00:09:30,724 --> 00:09:31,104
Lumen.

94
00:09:31,104 --> 00:09:35,097
The power of AI agents, made friendly for everyone on the team.
